1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
Welcome to School Physics, Explained.

2
00:00:02,690 --> 00:00:07,916
This is High School Physics, Unit 4,
Circular Motion and Gravity. In this

3
00:00:07,916 --> 00:00:10,135
video, we'll cover four topics.

4
00:00:10,585 --> 00:00:14,404
Uniform Circular Motion and Centripetal
Acceleration.

5
00:00:14,854 --> 00:00:17,563
Centripetal Force in Real Situations.

6
00:00:18,013 --> 00:00:20,659
Newton's Law of Universal Gravitation.

7
00:00:21,109 --> 00:00:22,495
Orbits and Satellites.

8
00:00:22,945 --> 00:00:24,161
Let's get started.

9
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:30,536
A car drives around a roundabout at a
steady thirty kilometers per hour.

10
00:00:30,986 --> 00:00:35,594
Its speed never changes. But is it
accelerating? Surprisingly, yes.

11
00:00:36,744 --> 00:00:41,373
Uniform circular motion means moving in a
circle at constant speed.

12
00:00:41,823 --> 00:00:45,834
The velocity always points along the
circle, tangent to the path.

13
00:00:46,284 --> 00:00:52,121
Its size stays the same, but its direction
keeps changing. And a changing velocity

14
00:00:52,121 --> 00:00:53,473
means acceleration.

15
00:00:54,623 --> 00:00:56,821
Which way does that acceleration point?

16
00:00:57,271 --> 00:01:02,180
Compare the velocity at two nearby
moments. The change in velocity points

17
00:01:02,180 --> 00:01:04,332
toward the center of the circle.

18
00:01:04,782 --> 00:01:10,011
So the acceleration always points toward
the center. We call it centripetal

19
00:01:10,011 --> 00:01:12,590
acceleration, meaning center seeking.

20
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:14,807
How big is it?

21
00:01:15,257 --> 00:01:20,633
a c equals v squared over r, where v is
the speed and r is the radius.

22
00:01:21,083 --> 00:01:27,062
Go twice as fast, and the acceleration is
four times as big. A tighter circle, with

23
00:01:27,062 --> 00:01:30,448
a smaller radius, also means more
acceleration.

24
00:01:31,598 --> 00:01:35,011
Often we know the period, T, the time for
one full lap.

25
00:01:35,461 --> 00:01:41,499
One lap is the circumference, two pi r. So
the speed is two pi r divided by T.

26
00:01:42,649 --> 00:01:47,948
Example: a car goes around a circle of
radius twenty meters at ten meters per

27
00:01:47,948 --> 00:01:48,430
second.

28
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:54,550
a c equals ten squared over twenty, which
is five meters per second squared, toward

29
00:01:54,550 --> 00:01:55,301
the center.

30
00:01:56,451 --> 00:01:57,923
Now try one yourself.

31
00:01:58,373 --> 00:02:04,110
A ball on a string moves in a circle of
radius zero point five meters, at four

32
00:02:04,110 --> 00:02:08,229
meters per second. What is its centripetal
acceleration?

33
00:02:08,679 --> 00:02:09,959
Pause the video now.

34
00:02:10,409 --> 00:02:15,701
a c equals four squared over zero point
five, which is thirty two meters per

35
00:02:15,701 --> 00:02:18,068
second squared, toward the center.

36
00:02:18,368 --> 00:02:23,104
Swing a ball on a string in a circle, then
let go. Which way does it fly?

37
00:02:23,554 --> 00:02:28,816
Not outward. It flies off in a straight
line, along the tangent. Something was

38
00:02:28,816 --> 00:02:31,447
pulling it toward the center all along.

39
00:02:32,597 --> 00:02:36,523
Newton's second law says any acceleration
needs a net force.

40
00:02:36,973 --> 00:02:43,185
So circular motion needs a net force
toward the center. Its size is m v squared

41
00:02:43,185 --> 00:02:43,735
over r.

42
00:02:44,185 --> 00:02:49,638
We call it the centripetal force. But it
is not a new kind of force. It's a job

43
00:02:49,638 --> 00:02:51,225
that a real force does.

44
00:02:52,375 --> 00:02:55,127
For a ball on a string, tension does the
job.

45
00:02:55,577 --> 00:03:00,798
For a car rounding a flat curve, static
friction between the tires and the road

46
00:03:00,798 --> 00:03:01,657
does the job.

47
00:03:02,107 --> 00:03:05,030
For the Moon orbiting Earth, gravity does
the job.

48
00:03:06,180 --> 00:03:09,700
What about the feeling of being thrown
outward in a turning car?

49
00:03:10,150 --> 00:03:14,739
That's your body trying to keep going
straight, which is inertia. There is no

50
00:03:14,739 --> 00:03:17,958
outward force pushing you. The door pushes
you inward.

51
00:03:19,108 --> 00:03:24,825
Example: a one thousand kilogram car takes
a flat curve of radius fifty meters at

52
00:03:24,825 --> 00:03:26,660
fifteen meters per second.

53
00:03:27,110 --> 00:03:32,692
The friction needed is m v squared over r.
That's one thousand times two hundred

54
00:03:32,692 --> 00:03:37,926
twenty five, divided by fifty, which is
four thousand five hundred newtons.

55
00:03:38,376 --> 00:03:43,663
If the road is icy and friction can't
supply that much, the car slides off along

56
00:03:43,663 --> 00:03:44,456
the tangent.

57
00:03:45,606 --> 00:03:49,019
One more: a bucket of water swung in a
vertical circle.

58
00:03:49,469 --> 00:03:54,627
At the top, both gravity and the normal
force from the bucket point down, toward

59
00:03:54,627 --> 00:03:55,336
the center.

60
00:03:55,786 --> 00:04:01,564
If the bucket moves fast enough, the water
stays in. The minimum speed at the top is

61
00:04:01,564 --> 00:04:03,146
the square root of g r.

62
00:04:04,296 --> 00:04:05,768
Now try one yourself.

63
00:04:06,218 --> 00:04:12,202
A zero point two kilogram ball moves in a
horizontal circle of radius zero point

64
00:04:12,202 --> 00:04:17,738
five meters at three meters per second.
What is the tension in the string?

65
00:04:18,188 --> 00:04:19,468
Pause the video now.

66
00:04:19,918 --> 00:04:26,344
T equals m v squared over r. That's zero
point two times nine, divided by zero

67
00:04:26,344 --> 00:04:30,051
point five, which is three point six
newtons.

68
00:04:30,351 --> 00:04:33,615
An apple falls from a tree. The Moon
circles the Earth.

69
00:04:34,065 --> 00:04:38,161
Newton's big idea was that these are the
same force: gravity.

70
00:04:39,311 --> 00:04:41,466
Every mass attracts every other mass.

71
00:04:41,916 --> 00:04:48,881
The force is F equals G times m one times
m two, divided by r squared. Here r is the

72
00:04:48,881 --> 00:04:51,452
distance between their centers.

73
00:04:51,902 --> 00:04:57,739
G is the gravitational constant, six point
six seven times ten to the minus eleven,

74
00:04:57,739 --> 00:05:03,366
in newton meters squared per kilogram
squared. It's tiny, which is why you don't

75
00:05:03,366 --> 00:05:05,406
feel pulled toward your desk.

76
00:05:06,556 --> 00:05:11,987
The force on each object is the same size,
pointing toward the other one. It's a

77
00:05:11,987 --> 00:05:13,617
Newton's third law pair.

78
00:05:14,067 --> 00:05:19,094
The Earth pulls the apple, and the apple
pulls the Earth just as hard. The Earth

79
00:05:19,094 --> 00:05:22,174
just has far more mass, so it barely
accelerates.

80
00:05:23,324 --> 00:05:25,094
This is an inverse square law.

81
00:05:25,544 --> 00:05:30,469
Double the distance, and the force drops
to one quarter. Triple it, and the force

82
00:05:30,469 --> 00:05:31,624
drops to one ninth.

83
00:05:32,774 --> 00:05:34,716
Gravity also explains g.

84
00:05:35,166 --> 00:05:40,641
At Earth's surface, g equals G times the
Earth's mass, divided by the Earth's

85
00:05:40,641 --> 00:05:45,903
radius squared. That works out to about
nine point eight meters per second

86
00:05:45,903 --> 00:05:46,472
squared.

87
00:05:46,922 --> 00:05:52,386
Go up to two Earth radii from the center,
and g drops to one quarter, about two

88
00:05:52,386 --> 00:05:53,493
point four five.

89
00:05:54,643 --> 00:05:56,115
Now try one yourself.

90
00:05:56,565 --> 00:06:01,382
Two objects attract each other with a
force of thirty six newtons. If the

91
00:06:01,382 --> 00:06:05,077
distance between them is tripled, what is
the new force?

92
00:06:05,527 --> 00:06:06,807
Pause the video now.

93
00:06:07,257 --> 00:06:13,123
Tripling the distance divides the force by
three squared, which is nine. Thirty six

94
00:06:13,123 --> 00:06:15,385
divided by nine is four newtons.

95
00:06:15,685 --> 00:06:20,144
Imagine a cannon on a very tall mountain,
firing a ball sideways.

96
00:06:20,594 --> 00:06:26,543
Fire it faster, and it lands farther away.
Fire it fast enough, and the ground curves

97
00:06:26,543 --> 00:06:31,303
away as fast as the ball falls. The ball
never lands. It's in orbit.

98
00:06:32,453 --> 00:06:37,402
So an orbiting satellite is falling all
the time. It just keeps missing the Earth.

99
00:06:37,852 --> 00:06:41,842
For a circular orbit, gravity provides the
centripetal force.

100
00:06:42,292 --> 00:06:48,990
G M m over r squared equals m v squared
over r. The satellite's mass cancels.

101
00:06:50,140 --> 00:06:51,356
Solve for the speed.

102
00:06:51,806 --> 00:06:57,856
v equals the square root of G M over r.
Here M is the mass of the planet, and r is

103
00:06:57,856 --> 00:07:00,660
the distance from the planet's center.

104
00:07:01,110 --> 00:07:05,696
Satellites closer to the planet move
faster. Farther ones move slower.

105
00:07:06,846 --> 00:07:10,729
The period is one lap, two pi r, divided
by the speed.

106
00:07:11,179 --> 00:07:16,213
The International Space Station orbits
about four hundred kilometers up, at

107
00:07:16,213 --> 00:07:21,516
nearly eight kilometers per second. It
goes around once every ninety minutes or

108
00:07:21,516 --> 00:07:21,718
so.

109
00:07:22,168 --> 00:07:28,245
A geostationary satellite is much farther
out. Its period is exactly one day, so it

110
00:07:28,245 --> 00:07:30,808
stays above the same spot on Earth.

111
00:07:31,958 --> 00:07:33,494
Why do astronauts float?

112
00:07:33,944 --> 00:07:38,973
Not because there's no gravity. At the
space station's height, gravity is still

113
00:07:38,973 --> 00:07:42,029
about ninety percent as strong as on the
ground.

114
00:07:42,479 --> 00:07:47,004
They float because they and the station
are falling together. Their apparent

115
00:07:47,004 --> 00:07:47,898
weight is zero.

116
00:07:49,048 --> 00:07:50,520
Now try one yourself.

117
00:07:50,970 --> 00:07:57,712
Satellite A orbits at radius r. Satellite
B orbits the same planet at radius four r.

118
00:07:57,712 --> 00:08:00,442
How does B's speed compare to A's?

119
00:08:00,892 --> 00:08:02,172
Pause the video now.

120
00:08:02,622 --> 00:08:08,674
v is proportional to one over the square
root of r. Four times the radius means one

121
00:08:08,674 --> 00:08:09,768
half the speed.

122
00:08:10,218 --> 00:08:16,290
That completes Unit 4. Next, a new way to
solve problems without forces: energy,

123
00:08:16,290 --> 00:08:17,429
work and power.

124
00:08:18,579 --> 00:08:23,414
If this lesson helped, subscribe to School
Physics, Explained, and tap the bell so

125
00:08:23,414 --> 00:08:25,064
you don't miss the next one.

